WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.340 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.470 align:middle line:84%
Additional information
about our license

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.870 align:middle line:84%
and MIT OpenCourseWare
in general

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.730 align:middle line:90%
is available at ocw.mit.edu.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:22.167 align:middle line:90%


00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:22.875 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Ready?

00:00:22.875 --> 00:00:26.076 align:middle line:90%


00:00:26.076 --> 00:00:27.050 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:32.409 align:middle line:84%
Well, welcome to
the third lecture.

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:35.060 align:middle line:84%
Quick review of what
we did last time--

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.590 align:middle line:90%
first slide.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:43.040 align:middle line:84%
We talked about
purification, basically

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.760 align:middle line:84%
at every level of the
hierarchy in complexity,

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.870 align:middle line:84%
from the elements all
the way up to organisms,

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.620 align:middle line:84%
and in particular, the
awesome purification one

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.630 align:middle line:84%
can get by serial dilution
down to single molecules--

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.030 align:middle line:84%
recombinant DNA
molecules, in this case,

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.940 align:middle line:90%
embedded in E. coli cells--

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:11.600 align:middle line:84%
and how this purification
led to a revolution,

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.210 align:middle line:84%
first in biochemistry,
then in recombinant DNA.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:17.720 align:middle line:84%
Molecular biology then led to
the Genome Project and systems

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.820 align:middle line:84%
biology, which brings us to
models of interconnections.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.775 align:middle line:84%
And to start in
with algorithms that

00:01:27.775 --> 00:01:29.150 align:middle line:84%
are useful in
systems biology, we

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.567 align:middle line:84%
start with one of the simplest
and most robust ones, which

00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:35.940 align:middle line:84%
is this genetic code, shown here
in the lower right-hand corner.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.550 align:middle line:84%
But as one looks at the huge
diversity in the tree of life,

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.210 align:middle line:84%
you find examples of
exceptions to almost everything

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.370 align:middle line:84%
you can come up with,
including the genetic code.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:49.310 align:middle line:84%
And we talked about
how to cherish

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.560 align:middle line:90%
the exceptions, as usual, here.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.680 align:middle line:84%
And then, getting back
to systems biology,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.590 align:middle line:84%
in terms of how one creates
qualitative and quantitative

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.540 align:middle line:84%
models from functional genomics
data and establishes evidence.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.700 align:middle line:84%
Finally, we ended on
mutations and selection,

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.419 align:middle line:84%
as we will do in all
three lectures here.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:10.610 align:middle line:84%
This lecture in
particular will focus

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.870 align:middle line:90%
on mutations and selection.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:16.190 align:middle line:90%
So what is today's menu?

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.460 align:middle line:84%
We'll start with
types of mutants

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:25.880 align:middle line:84%
and how they're represented
for bioinformatic purposes.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:32.450 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about the three
main methods by which mutations

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.300 align:middle line:84%
occur, drift, and
select so that you

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:40.220 align:middle line:84%
can determine the frequency
of different alleles

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.140 align:middle line:90%
in populations.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.840 align:middle line:84%
In doing that, we'll rely on our
friend from the first lecture,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.260 align:middle line:84%
the binomial distribution, in
the context of an exponentially

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.970 align:middle line:90%
growing population.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.030 align:middle line:90%


00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:00.920 align:middle line:84%
Then, give you some
very practical training

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.850 align:middle line:84%
here on association studies,
where we illustrate it

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:09.500 align:middle line:84%
with a very important
example of HIV resistance

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.800 align:middle line:84%
and a very useful
statistic, easily as

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.900 align:middle line:84%
useful as the binomial
and the Gaussian--

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.610 align:middle line:90%
the chi-squared statistic.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.950 align:middle line:84%
And then, we'll continue
to talk about association

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:24.020 align:middle line:84%
in the context of
causative alleles

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.990 align:middle line:84%
and importance of
getting haplotypes,

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.840 align:middle line:84%
and then technologies
that are required

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.340 align:middle line:84%
that have been used to get
the framework first genome

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.500 align:middle line:84%
and how one might
change strategy

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.170 align:middle line:84%
in getting subsequent
genomes in order

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:45.150 align:middle line:84%
to make cost-effective these
very large association studies.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.060 align:middle line:84%
And finally, in the
context of that,

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.120 align:middle line:84%
we'll talk about random
and systematic errors

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.730 align:middle line:84%
in more detail so you get
lots of examples and ways

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.430 align:middle line:84%
of dealing with it
computationally.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:04:00.030 align:middle line:84%
So just to go from our brief
discussion about our friend,

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:06.500 align:middle line:84%
the 100% DNA sequence identity,
or amino acid sequence identity

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:13.790 align:middle line:84%
that you might find in the lens
protein and enolase enzyme,

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:17.930 align:middle line:84%
we can see that, even at
100%, you can find differences

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:18.600 align:middle line:90%
in function.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.410 align:middle line:84%
And so functional measures
are a good adjunct to DNA

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:26.870 align:middle line:90%
or amino acids' identity.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:31.280 align:middle line:84%
At 99.9% identity, we're
talking about the level

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.650 align:middle line:84%
of single-nucleotide
polymorphisms

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:39.140 align:middle line:84%
that might exist between any
two positions in your mother

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.360 align:middle line:84%
and your father's
chromosomes in your body,

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.280 align:middle line:84%
or the differences between
one of your genomes

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:44.197 align:middle line:90%
and one of my genomes.

00:04:44.197 --> 00:04:46.370 align:middle line:84%
About once every
kilobase, there will be

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:49.280 align:middle line:90%
a polymorphism, a difference.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.450 align:middle line:84%
It's often a single
nucleotide, an A for a G.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.643 align:middle line:84%
Then, as we go to
98% differences,

00:04:56.643 --> 00:04:58.310 align:middle line:84%
then we're talking
about the differences

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.130 align:middle line:84%
between one of our genomes
and a chimpanzee genome--

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:04.140 align:middle line:84%
in other words, a completely
different genus and species.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.040 align:middle line:84%
However, by the criterion we
mentioned in a previous lecture

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.250 align:middle line:84%
about bacterial
definitions of species,

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.860 align:middle line:84%
those would be almost
identical, and you'd

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:19.820 align:middle line:84%
have to go to identities
less than 70% in order

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.600 align:middle line:84%
to call it a new
bacterial species.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.320 align:middle line:84%
So you can see that this
is a very soft number, very

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.080 align:middle line:84%
dependent upon the context and
the branch of the tree of life

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:31.340 align:middle line:90%
that you're working with.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:37.670 align:middle line:84%
Then, we have sequence
homology and very

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.852 align:middle line:84%
distant homologs which are
only detectable, as we'll

00:05:40.852 --> 00:05:42.560 align:middle line:84%
see when we get to
the proteomics section

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.210 align:middle line:84%
of this course, by
three-dimensional structures,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:48.080 align:middle line:90%
not by sequence.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.650 align:middle line:84%
And that switchover--
sequence homology

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.070 align:middle line:84%
will be the topic
of next lecture,

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:57.860 align:middle line:84%
and the very distant
one in 3D structures

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.450 align:middle line:90%
will be later, in proteomics.

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:05.490 align:middle line:84%
But that occurs at
about 25% to 30%.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:10.070 align:middle line:84%
This is just a reminder
and an introductory slide

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:11.280 align:middle line:90%
to the next slide.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.290 align:middle line:84%
We have different
phenotypic effects

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.420 align:middle line:84%
due to different
types of mutations.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:21.570 align:middle line:84%
And ignoring the phenotypes
that we talked about before,

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:23.330 align:middle line:90%
you have the types of mutations.

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:28.520 align:middle line:84%
Classes are null mutations,
dosage, conditional mutations,

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:31.460 align:middle line:84%
gain-of-function, altered
ligand specificity.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:34.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, that's in broad
terms, colloquially.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:38.390 align:middle line:84%
But how is this achieved
on a more molecular level,

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.580 align:middle line:84%
and how do we
represent it compactly

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:43.650 align:middle line:90%
for bioinformatic discussion?

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:48.050 align:middle line:90%
So we have single substitutions.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:52.190 align:middle line:84%
These can be a single
base par, an AT base pair,

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:55.880 align:middle line:84%
to a CG base pair, or a GC
base pair to a TA base pair,

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:58.400 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.000 align:middle line:84%
There can be deletions
and duplications.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.010 align:middle line:90%


00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:05.180 align:middle line:90%
These can range.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.720 align:middle line:84%
The deletions and duplications
be as large as a chromosome

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.580 align:middle line:84%
or as small as a
single base pair.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:13.730 align:middle line:84%
You can delete that A rather
than change it into a C,

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:17.673 align:middle line:90%
and that would be one base pair.

00:07:17.673 --> 00:07:19.340 align:middle line:84%
So when you delete
an entire chromosome,

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.260 align:middle line:90%
that's called aneuploidy.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:24.950 align:middle line:84%
The example in the previous
slide was trisomy 21.

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:27.770 align:middle line:84%
If you remove that
chromosome 21 instead

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:31.980 align:middle line:84%
of having three copies of
it, it would be monosomy.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:35.810 align:middle line:84%
And these are huge
consequences even

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:38.510 align:middle line:84%
though they're fairly
subtle changes in dosage.

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:41.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, a special class of
deletion and duplication

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:45.140 align:middle line:84%
occurs when you have a
tandem repeat of a sequence.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:50.360 align:middle line:84%
When you have anywhere from
a single base repeat, AAAAA,

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:54.890 align:middle line:84%
or dinucleotide,
trinucleotide, all the way up,

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:59.180 align:middle line:84%
those have a very
high tendency for

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:04.790 align:middle line:84%
both forward and reverse
mutation, both deletions

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:07.580 align:middle line:84%
and duplications, because
at the very small level,

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:13.160 align:middle line:84%
you have polymerase slippage
and other microscopic events.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.770 align:middle line:84%
And then, at the
larger level, you

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:19.400 align:middle line:84%
have some kind of
recombination, often homologous

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.440 align:middle line:84%
recombination, that occurs
that cause deletions

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:23.750 align:middle line:84%
and duplications
of tandem repeats.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:26.720 align:middle line:90%
An inversion here.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.440 align:middle line:84%
We're representing a
complicated chemical event which

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.270 align:middle line:84%
involves double-stranded
DNA going

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:34.787 align:middle line:84%
5 prime to 3 prime on the top
strand, from left to right,

00:08:34.787 --> 00:08:36.620 align:middle line:84%
and 5 prime to 3 prime
on the bottom strand,

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:38.413 align:middle line:90%
going from right to left.

00:08:38.413 --> 00:08:40.080 align:middle line:84%
And you're taking a
little chunk of that

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:42.020 align:middle line:84%
and flipping it so that
you break some bonds

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.090 align:middle line:90%
and remake them.

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.870 align:middle line:84%
All the 3 prime is
conserved, but you've

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:50.570 align:middle line:84%
done a reverse complement
of the top strand

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.640 align:middle line:90%
and the bottom strand.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:56.330 align:middle line:84%
In that reverse complement,
you basically turn Cs into Gs

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:57.890 align:middle line:90%
and change the order.

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:04.130 align:middle line:84%
This is sometimes abbreviated
for simple genetic description.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:06.200 align:middle line:90%
Let's say we inverted CDE.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:10.040 align:middle line:84%
You might change the case or
add primes to it, color it.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.540 align:middle line:84%
In some way or
another, you indicate

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:13.550 align:middle line:84%
that it's now a
reverse complement,

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:16.880 align:middle line:84%
and of course, the gene order
or the DNA segment order

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.880 align:middle line:84%
is reversed to indicate
that inversion.

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:22.400 align:middle line:90%


00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.130 align:middle line:84%
Translocations, insertions,
and recombination

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:30.560 align:middle line:84%
have in common that you
will make a break somewhere,

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:33.860 align:middle line:84%
and then you will bring
in a new piece of DNA,

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:37.610 align:middle line:84%
just like the inversion,
where you inverted in place.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:41.420 align:middle line:84%
Here, it involves something
acting at more of a distance.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.540 align:middle line:84%
You'll break between
B and C and X and Y,

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:51.170 align:middle line:84%
and you'll, in this case, do
a reciprocal translocation

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:55.280 align:middle line:84%
where you conserve all
the DNA, and A and B

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:59.070 align:middle line:90%
is now next to Y and Z.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:04.350 align:middle line:84%
An insertion is like that,
but now the DNA need not

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:06.460 align:middle line:90%
be reciprocal in any sense.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:09.360 align:middle line:84%
It can come in, more or
less, from outer space.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.555 align:middle line:90%
Here, it came from Greece.

00:10:10.555 --> 00:10:14.370 align:middle line:90%


00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:19.500 align:middle line:84%
It can be a transposable
element that

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:21.660 align:middle line:84%
came in from who knows
where and inserted in

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:23.580 align:middle line:90%
between the A and B.

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:24.510 align:middle line:90%
Recombination.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:28.140 align:middle line:84%
Here, I illustrated this
homologous recombination.

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:30.810 align:middle line:84%
And you can have an
non-homologous combination,

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:34.150 align:middle line:84%
but this is by far the most
common and interesting.

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:36.000 align:middle line:84%
Even some non-homologous
recombination

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.920 align:middle line:84%
involves short
regions of homology

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.380 align:middle line:84%
where you basically have
either two sister chromosomes

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:46.680 align:middle line:84%
or homologs from
mother and father

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.590 align:middle line:84%
or paralogs within the
genome where you've

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:51.990 align:middle line:84%
got to duplicate a
gene that might even

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:53.910 align:middle line:90%
be on the same chromosome arm.

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:56.790 align:middle line:84%
And now, just as with the
deletions and duplication,

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.790 align:middle line:84%
where you have tandem repeats,
if you have repeats anywhere

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:03.720 align:middle line:84%
in the genome, you can
take a small difference.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:05.430 align:middle line:84%
We're emphasizing the
similarities here,

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:06.972 align:middle line:84%
but they have a few
small differences

00:11:06.972 --> 00:11:08.700 align:middle line:90%
that allow you to track them.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.770 align:middle line:84%
Those can be exchanged
by single-strand or

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:16.320 align:middle line:84%
double-stranded DNA,
various chemistries,

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.950 align:middle line:84%
and you can either do a nice
reciprocal exchange here,

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:22.320 align:middle line:84%
where you preserve all
the DNA and the little C

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:26.880 align:middle line:84%
gets replacing the big C by
some kind of crossover between D

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.220 align:middle line:84%
and E, somewhere
between C and F,

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:32.370 align:middle line:84%
or you can have a nonreciprocal
exchange where you pick up

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:35.530 align:middle line:84%
a little bit of C
and duplicate it,

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:38.880 align:middle line:84%
and now there's no big C
left over and only little C.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:41.490 align:middle line:90%
Called gene conversion.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:42.870 align:middle line:90%
But you get the idea.

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:45.420 align:middle line:84%
These are a fairly
exhaustive list

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:48.690 align:middle line:84%
of the kinds of simple,
elementary mutations

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:49.290 align:middle line:90%
you can have.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.840 align:middle line:84%
Now, you can pile these
in various combinations

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.250 align:middle line:84%
with each other, and over
long periods of time,

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:58.020 align:middle line:84%
you can get completely
new sequences.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:01.680 align:middle line:84%
There's even ways that you can
get de novo synthesis of DNA,

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:04.380 align:middle line:84%
such as mechanisms of
terminal transferase, which

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.060 align:middle line:90%
is used-- yes?

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:07.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What are
the [INAUDIBLE]??

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:09.900 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Oh, we're going
to get to that a little bit

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.525 align:middle line:84%
later on, but I can
give you a preview.

00:12:11.525 --> 00:12:14.430 align:middle line:90%


00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:18.390 align:middle line:84%
In human genome, these point
mutations occurred about 10

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:24.270 align:middle line:84%
to the minus 8 per base
pair per generation.

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.970 align:middle line:84%
Deletions and duplications,
especially in tandem regions,

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:30.630 align:middle line:84%
can be as much as six orders
of magnitude higher-frequency

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:33.300 align:middle line:90%
than that.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:37.650 align:middle line:84%
So there's a huge variation from
position to position and types

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:40.260 align:middle line:84%
of alleles that will
determine the mutation rate,

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:42.720 align:middle line:84%
and you should be
quite aware of that

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:47.790 align:middle line:84%
as you go through various
computational exercises.

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:55.650 align:middle line:84%
This is just a bit of commonly
accepted nomenclature.

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:58.530 align:middle line:84%
Mutations and polymorphisms
are basically same thing.

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:02.520 align:middle line:84%
There are differences
between you and me.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:05.978 align:middle line:84%
They become
polymorphisms, meaning

00:13:05.978 --> 00:13:08.520 align:middle line:84%
when they are common alleles,
when their frequency is greater

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:10.390 align:middle line:90%
than 1% in a population.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:14.330 align:middle line:84%
This is common in the
human genome community.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:16.440 align:middle line:90%
It may differ for other ones.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:20.550 align:middle line:84%
But this is roughly where
a mutation which is rare--

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:22.630 align:middle line:84%
less than 1%-- becomes
a polymorphism,

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:25.950 align:middle line:84%
which is frequent--
more than 1%.

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:30.390 align:middle line:84%
As a counterpoint to that, I
would say that there is a good

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:34.380 align:middle line:84%
chance that every possible
single-nucleotide polymorphism

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:39.240 align:middle line:84%
that could exist does exist in
a population as large as ours

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:43.140 align:middle line:84%
and mutation rates which
are modest but still,

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:46.590 align:middle line:84%
over long periods of time, allow
the accumulation of mutations

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:49.650 align:middle line:84%
such that, rather than
having maybe 3 million common

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:52.800 align:middle line:84%
single-nucleotide
polymorphisms, or SNPs,

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:54.696 align:middle line:90%
we might have 12 billion--

00:13:54.696 --> 00:13:55.980 align:middle line:90%
one at every position.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.930 align:middle line:84%
And in a later
slide, we'll actually

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:04.140 align:middle line:84%
go through and calculate
crudely why the frequency should

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:06.150 align:middle line:84%
be around 10 to
the minus 5 and why

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:09.780 align:middle line:84%
there should be about
10,000 of us representing

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:13.350 align:middle line:84%
each of these
so-called rare alleles.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:16.515 align:middle line:84%
They're rare individually,
but they're common as a group.

00:14:16.515 --> 00:14:18.962 align:middle line:90%


00:14:18.962 --> 00:14:21.420 align:middle line:84%
It will make a difference as
to whether we're talking about

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:25.980 align:middle line:84%
whether these polymorphisms are
linked to your favorite trait

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:28.230 align:middle line:84%
or whether they
actually cause it,

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:32.214 align:middle line:84%
whether they're
part of the cause.

00:14:32.214 --> 00:14:36.730 align:middle line:84%
No particular one can be said
to be 1-to-1 with a cause.

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:41.130 align:middle line:84%
It's all a collection of
mutations and environment.

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:42.180 align:middle line:90%
Now, haplotypes.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:43.950 align:middle line:90%
What do we mean by haplotypes?

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:45.960 align:middle line:84%
We've introduced SNPs,
single-nucleotide

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:46.980 align:middle line:90%
polymorphisms.

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:51.540 align:middle line:84%
If you have a SNP
that, let's say,

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:56.130 align:middle line:84%
is involved in
causing a APOE4 we'll

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:59.130 align:middle line:84%
introduce in just a
moment, an allele that

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:03.840 align:middle line:84%
is associated with increased
risk of Alzheimer's disease--

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:09.090 align:middle line:84%
and let's say that protein has
a known change in the protein

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:13.140 align:middle line:84%
three-dimensional
structure, and you

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:16.860 align:middle line:84%
can call that the bad allele,
or the associated allele.

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:20.730 align:middle line:84%
Now if that protein
were expressed

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:23.400 align:middle line:84%
at a low level-- for example,
if you had a promoter mutation

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.920 align:middle line:84%
or enhancer mutation--
then that haplotype,

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.670 align:middle line:84%
that combination of promoter
mutation and protein mutation,

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:33.810 align:middle line:84%
is a more significant predictor
than either one of them

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:34.480 align:middle line:90%
separately.

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:36.480 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that they're
on the same chromosome

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:39.720 align:middle line:84%
is important because if the
promoter or enhancer that

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:43.920 align:middle line:84%
determines the level
of the protein in cis,

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:47.410 align:middle line:84%
meaning on the same DNA, or in
trans, makes a huge difference.

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:49.200 align:middle line:84%
So that's what
haplotyping is all about.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:51.690 align:middle line:84%
It's determining
what mutations are

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:57.330 align:middle line:84%
in cis on the same DNA in
order to make associations

00:15:57.330 --> 00:16:00.390 align:middle line:84%
biologically meaningful and,
in terms of systems biology,

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:01.890 align:middle line:84%
interpreting them,
in terms of what

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:08.490 align:middle line:84%
you know about regulatory
elements and protein elements.

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:14.070 align:middle line:84%
These haplotypes can be inferred
indirectly from diploid data

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:21.690 align:middle line:84%
from how alleles segregate in
small families or in siblings

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:23.100 align:middle line:90%
and so forth.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:27.543 align:middle line:84%
Or, easier to think about
and probably more accurate,

00:16:27.543 --> 00:16:29.460 align:middle line:84%
in general, especially
with limited data sets,

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:32.580 align:middle line:90%
is direct observation.

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:34.470 align:middle line:84%
The most extreme
direct observation

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:38.740 align:middle line:84%
is you pull out a DNA molecule
that has, say, your promoter

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:42.060 align:middle line:84%
mutation in your putative
protein mutation,

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:45.945 align:middle line:84%
or just a series of
linked polymorphisms.

00:16:45.945 --> 00:16:48.240 align:middle line:84%
By cloning out or
otherwise physically

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:52.320 align:middle line:84%
isolating that DNA molecule,
you can sequence it,

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.520 align:middle line:90%
and you can determine.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:57.233 align:middle line:84%
By definition, if they're
all in the same sequence,

00:16:57.233 --> 00:16:58.650 align:middle line:84%
then they're on
the same molecule.

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:00.900 align:middle line:84%
But you have to be careful
about the sequencing method

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.270 align:middle line:84%
that you're using there
and the specific cloning

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:06.480 align:middle line:84%
and/or physical separation
method, because there are

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:09.300 align:middle line:84%
certain methods where you can
get a chimeric sequence either

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:13.630 align:middle line:84%
due to cloning of two
species together or somehow

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:17.550 align:middle line:84%
disassembling them
through bioinformatics.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:19.770 align:middle line:84%
You can also directly
observe it when

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:26.550 align:middle line:84%
you go through the genetic
processes of mitosis, which

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:29.520 align:middle line:84%
we've talked about before, which
is that, as the cells divide,

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.870 align:middle line:84%
they split up the
chromosomes, or meiosis,

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:34.050 align:middle line:84%
which is the process by
which they get prepared

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:37.110 align:middle line:84%
for recombination, which we'll
come back to in a little while.

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:40.020 align:middle line:90%


00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:42.660 align:middle line:84%
So when you do want
to do it by linkage,

00:17:42.660 --> 00:17:44.700 align:middle line:84%
you want to do a
direct observation.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:52.740 align:middle line:84%
And the best way to
follow the haplotype

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:59.940 align:middle line:84%
is, if there is a difference
at every position in both

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:04.410 align:middle line:84%
the parents and the child
inherits those differences,

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:06.240 align:middle line:90%
then it's called informative.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:11.340 align:middle line:84%
If the parents happen to share
a single-nucleotide polymorphism

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.250 align:middle line:84%
even though the child is a
heterozygote for one of them,

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:17.580 align:middle line:84%
it could be that the parents
have additional alleles that

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:19.470 align:middle line:84%
can confuse things, and
it's not informative.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:22.260 align:middle line:84%
But the point is, if you
have enough single-nucleotide

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:25.950 align:middle line:84%
polymorphisms, you can do
a case-control study where

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:28.770 align:middle line:84%
you have lots of children
that are affected for whatever

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:30.840 align:middle line:84%
trait you're interested
in and, hopefully,

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:33.420 align:middle line:84%
a close-to-equal number which
are in the control group which

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:34.193 align:middle line:90%
do not have it.

00:18:34.193 --> 00:18:36.360 align:middle line:84%
An example of that-- and
I'm just trying to give you

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:37.890 align:middle line:84%
a flavor for this
and where to look,

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.260 align:middle line:84%
rather than to completely
empower you on this,

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:42.510 align:middle line:84%
because that would be an
entire separate course--

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:45.300 align:middle line:84%
but you can look
for association.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.580 align:middle line:84%
And you have to worry
about things like structure

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.460 align:middle line:84%
and admixture, where you've had
populations that have developed

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:52.380 align:middle line:84%
independently in different
parts of the world

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:56.370 align:middle line:84%
and have been
randomly mixing, which

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:59.260 align:middle line:84%
is part of the model in
these separate populations.

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:02.220 align:middle line:84%
But then, you bring them
together very recently,

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:06.630 align:middle line:84%
and now it's no longer
fair to model it

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:10.440 align:middle line:84%
as if it were a uniformly
mixing population.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:15.480 align:middle line:84%
And we'll refer
throughout the course

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:20.110 align:middle line:84%
to the null hypothesis, which
is the thing that you're

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:22.420 align:middle line:84%
trying to rule out,
and the probability

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:24.550 align:middle line:84%
refers to the
probability that you can

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:26.292 align:middle line:90%
reject this null hypothesis.

00:19:26.292 --> 00:19:28.000 align:middle line:84%
And in this case,
you're trying to reject

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.430 align:middle line:84%
that the allele frequencies
in the candidate locus,

00:19:30.430 --> 00:19:33.070 align:middle line:84%
whatever you picked,
do not depend

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:35.350 align:middle line:84%
on the phenotype within
the subpopulations.

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:39.160 align:middle line:84%
And that's the way that they
deal with these case studies.

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:44.920 align:middle line:84%
Now, what are some of the
motivations for studying

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:47.800 align:middle line:84%
either individual
polymorphisms or haplotypes--

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:50.230 align:middle line:84%
combinations of
polymorphisms can affect

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:52.390 align:middle line:90%
the activity of a protein?

00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:55.930 align:middle line:84%
Now, I could use hundreds
of different examples

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:59.590 align:middle line:84%
of well-established
and useful examples.

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:04.930 align:middle line:84%
But here's one that, hopefully,
will hit a resonating chord

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:07.630 align:middle line:84%
in the sense that these
are actually used now

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:11.170 align:middle line:84%
in certain clinical
settings, and certainly

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:16.930 align:middle line:84%
in clinical research, to ask
whether a patient population,

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:19.420 align:middle line:84%
either in the process
of developing a new drug

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:28.600 align:middle line:84%
or using an established drug to
keep the patient toxicity down

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:30.400 align:middle line:90%
and the effectiveness up.

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:34.600 align:middle line:84%
And so in the far
left-hand column,

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.550 align:middle line:90%
is the gene or enzyme affected?

00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:38.230 align:middle line:84%
And then, in the
middle is our examples

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:41.120 align:middle line:84%
of drugs which interact
with this enzyme.

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:42.520 align:middle line:84%
And then, the
quantitative effect

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:46.180 align:middle line:90%
is in the far right-hand side.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:49.990 align:middle line:84%
For example, thiopurine
methyltransferase

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:54.550 align:middle line:84%
is something which, if you have
a large amount of the activity,

00:20:54.550 --> 00:20:58.510 align:middle line:84%
whether you have, say,
a very active enzyme

00:20:58.510 --> 00:21:01.750 align:middle line:84%
and/or a very active
promoter element that

00:21:01.750 --> 00:21:06.160 align:middle line:84%
causes high levels of it, then
these various chemotherapeutics

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:07.675 align:middle line:84%
that are used for
fighting cancers--

00:21:07.675 --> 00:21:10.455 align:middle line:90%


00:21:10.455 --> 00:21:12.080 align:middle line:84%
the amount of them
need to be adjusted.

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:13.900 align:middle line:84%
So you have lots of
the methyltransferase.

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:17.860 align:middle line:84%
That means you have to give lots
of the drug, or else your drug

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:21.490 align:middle line:84%
study will fail, or your
patient will succumb to cancer

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:22.750 align:middle line:90%
because it's ineffective.

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:24.460 align:middle line:90%
You haven't added enough.

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:33.640 align:middle line:84%
The thiopurine methyltransferase
is overcoming the drug.

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:37.360 align:middle line:90%


00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:45.820 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if
you have very low levels

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:48.760 align:middle line:84%
of the modifying enzyme,
you want to lower the dose,

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:51.970 align:middle line:90%
or else you'll have toxicity.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:54.340 align:middle line:84%
So that's an example
that's actually

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:56.080 align:middle line:84%
being used in
clinical situations

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:59.620 align:middle line:84%
where you can use
the information

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:04.000 align:middle line:84%
to adjust drug
levels or to stratify

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:07.060 align:middle line:84%
your patient population
so that you put patients

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:10.360 align:middle line:84%
into different classes or
exclude them from the study

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.820 align:middle line:84%
altogether because you
know that the drug will

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:14.390 align:middle line:90%
have some bad effect.

00:22:14.390 --> 00:22:17.380 align:middle line:84%
And this, hopefully,
decreases the costs.

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:19.660 align:middle line:84%
On the downside to
pharmaceutical development,

00:22:19.660 --> 00:22:21.850 align:middle line:84%
if you do stratify
your patient population

00:22:21.850 --> 00:22:25.480 align:middle line:84%
and you make it through the
drug study with that caveat,

00:22:25.480 --> 00:22:29.110 align:middle line:84%
then the FDA will require
that you put that proviso,

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:32.050 align:middle line:84%
and that makes the size
of the population that

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:36.520 align:middle line:84%
will be buying the drug smaller,
since the costs of developing

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:38.950 align:middle line:90%
the drug are fairly fixed.

00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:42.190 align:middle line:90%
It decreases the profit.

00:22:42.190 --> 00:22:45.880 align:middle line:84%
Now, I pointed
out that there may

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:48.580 align:middle line:84%
be a very large number
of rare single-nucleotide

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:49.570 align:middle line:90%
polymorphisms.

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:52.450 align:middle line:84%
But in terms of
common ones, we're

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:56.010 align:middle line:84%
getting pretty close to
saturating the common ones.

00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:58.010 align:middle line:84%
And there are databases
of these just like there

00:22:58.010 --> 00:22:59.440 align:middle line:84%
are databases of
almost everything you

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:01.315 align:middle line:84%
can imagine, some of
them better than others.

00:23:01.315 --> 00:23:05.140 align:middle line:90%


00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:07.750 align:middle line:84%
The common ones will,
of course, be the ones

00:23:07.750 --> 00:23:10.900 align:middle line:84%
that either are
neutral, with respect

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:12.850 align:middle line:90%
to their phenotypic effect.

00:23:12.850 --> 00:23:14.410 align:middle line:84%
That is to say,
it doesn't really

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:20.650 align:middle line:84%
matter whether they're one
common allele or the other one.

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:23.320 align:middle line:84%
The one allele might be at
30% and the other one at 70%,

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:26.570 align:middle line:84%
but they're both fairly neutral
with respect to function.

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:31.810 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be that they both
provide different advantages

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:32.890 align:middle line:90%
in different scenarios.

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:35.290 align:middle line:84%
Or the heterozygote, where
you have one over the other,

00:23:35.290 --> 00:23:36.748 align:middle line:84%
provides some
advantage, and that's

00:23:36.748 --> 00:23:38.590 align:middle line:90%
kept it in the population.

00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:40.210 align:middle line:84%
But it's unlikely
that they're highly

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:43.060 align:middle line:84%
deleterious because the
highly deleterious alleles are

00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:43.840 align:middle line:90%
going to be rare.

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:45.382 align:middle line:84%
They're going to be
selected against.

00:23:45.382 --> 00:23:47.810 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to fully
model that in just a moment.

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:54.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's say that, somehow,
anybody who wanted their genome

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:57.115 align:middle line:84%
could have their
genome tomorrow.

00:23:57.115 --> 00:23:58.990 align:middle line:84%
You could have your
complete genome sequence.

00:23:58.990 --> 00:24:03.250 align:middle line:84%
How would you, then, as
computational biologists,

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:07.217 align:middle line:84%
prioritize the single-nucleotide
polymorphisms you find

00:24:07.217 --> 00:24:08.800 align:middle line:84%
in there, relative
to the whole genome

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:10.120 align:middle line:90%
sequence, which is in GenBank?

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:12.078 align:middle line:84%
Now, this would be an
excellent project for you

00:24:12.078 --> 00:24:14.620 align:middle line:90%
to do for the term project.

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:16.405 align:middle line:84%
But what you might
say, first of all--

00:24:16.405 --> 00:24:19.950 align:middle line:90%


00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:22.250 align:middle line:84%
what single-nucleotide
polymorphisms

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:24.800 align:middle line:84%
would you throw
out, for example,

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:26.858 align:middle line:84%
or put low on your
priority list?

00:24:26.858 --> 00:24:29.150 align:middle line:84%
Or which ones would you put
high on your priority list?

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:32.450 align:middle line:90%


00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:32.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:34.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Introns
would be low-priority.

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:37.198 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK, introns.

00:24:37.198 --> 00:24:39.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Maybe this is a
very simplistic thing to say,

00:24:39.490 --> 00:24:43.600 align:middle line:84%
but I guess [INAUDIBLE]
matter if they're

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:46.005 align:middle line:90%
different from one another.

00:24:46.005 --> 00:24:49.070 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:50.590 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: That's fine.

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:53.470 align:middle line:84%
Everybody has their own pet part
of the genome they don't like.

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:55.155 align:middle line:84%
Introns almost sunk
the Genome Project.

00:24:55.155 --> 00:24:56.780 align:middle line:84%
They said, why are
we going to sequence

00:24:56.780 --> 00:25:00.070 align:middle line:84%
the 98% of the genome that
doesn't code for proteins?

00:25:00.070 --> 00:25:02.200 align:middle line:84%
Fortunately, we went ahead
and sequenced it anyway.

00:25:02.200 --> 00:25:05.080 align:middle line:84%
Another favorite thing
that people mention

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:06.860 align:middle line:90%
is repetitive DNA.

00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:08.360 align:middle line:84%
That was another
part of the genome.

00:25:08.360 --> 00:25:09.860 align:middle line:84%
But they didn't
actually sequence it

00:25:09.860 --> 00:25:13.480 align:middle line:84%
from Drosophila,
the repetitive DNA.

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:20.702 align:middle line:84%
And it's considered
also not protein coding.

00:25:20.702 --> 00:25:22.660 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to give
you a couple of examples,

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:24.743 align:middle line:84%
as we go through here, to
illustrate other points,

00:25:24.743 --> 00:25:27.280 align:middle line:84%
but also to illustrate that
repetitive regions that are not

00:25:27.280 --> 00:25:29.170 align:middle line:84%
in protein-coding
regions, whether introns

00:25:29.170 --> 00:25:31.900 align:middle line:84%
or other non-coding
regions, can be important.

00:25:31.900 --> 00:25:32.958 align:middle line:90%
And here's an example.

00:25:32.958 --> 00:25:34.750 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the most
repetitive elements

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:35.583 align:middle line:90%
in the human genome.

00:25:35.583 --> 00:25:37.420 align:middle line:90%
It's called an Alu repeat.

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:41.380 align:middle line:84%
As those of you who have done
bioinformatics before realize,

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:44.710 align:middle line:84%
it's the bane of our existence,
in terms of assembling

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.040 align:middle line:90%
and searching and so forth.

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:49.930 align:middle line:84%
But here's an example
of a single base

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:51.550 align:middle line:90%
mutation in this repeat.

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:54.250 align:middle line:84%
There's about 500,000 copies
of this in the human genome

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:55.070 align:middle line:90%
scattered about.

00:25:55.070 --> 00:25:57.670 align:middle line:84%
It's called an intersperse
repeat as a consequence.

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:00.400 align:middle line:84%
And this A-to-G
transmission is found

00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:03.260 align:middle line:84%
upstream from the
myeloperoxidase enzyme-encoding

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:03.760 align:middle line:90%
gene.

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:07.940 align:middle line:90%


00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:11.480 align:middle line:84%
So how do we find out whether
this is important in any sense?

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:13.480 align:middle line:84%
First of all, the
observation is that it

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:17.530 align:middle line:84%
is associated with several-fold
less transcriptional activity.

00:26:17.530 --> 00:26:21.490 align:middle line:90%


00:26:21.490 --> 00:26:26.680 align:middle line:84%
That particular position
creates or destroys

00:26:26.680 --> 00:26:30.250 align:middle line:84%
binding sites for these
transcription elements,

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:32.470 align:middle line:84%
and that might be the
reason that it has

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:34.100 align:middle line:90%
lower transcriptional activity.

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:36.850 align:middle line:84%
And finally, it is
over-represented

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:40.820 align:middle line:90%
in a particular type of cancer.

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:43.300 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
go through the ways

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:49.840 align:middle line:84%
that we take an observation,
like a polymorphism,

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:54.370 align:middle line:84%
move to an association,
like here, with cancer, then

00:26:54.370 --> 00:26:56.560 align:middle line:84%
take it to a mechanism,
such as here,

00:26:56.560 --> 00:26:59.380 align:middle line:84%
with the transcriptional
regulators.

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:01.160 align:middle line:84%
I think that's
what this is about.

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:03.580 align:middle line:84%
It's not sufficient to
observe the polymorphism.

00:27:03.580 --> 00:27:06.190 align:middle line:84%
You can't say, a
priori, whether it's

00:27:06.190 --> 00:27:08.908 align:middle line:84%
important or not, whether the
Alu repeats are not conserved.

00:27:08.908 --> 00:27:10.450 align:middle line:84%
That's another thing
that people say.

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:13.600 align:middle line:84%
Throw out all the
nonconserved single-nucleotide

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:14.890 align:middle line:90%
polymorphisms.

00:27:14.890 --> 00:27:15.700 align:middle line:90%
It's not conserved.

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:19.210 align:middle line:84%
It's not present in
mouse, for example.

00:27:19.210 --> 00:27:22.210 align:middle line:84%
It's non-coding,
and it's repetitive.

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:24.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, in addition to
the types of mutations,

00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:27.760 align:middle line:84%
we have the modes of
inheritance-- that is to say,

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:33.868 align:middle line:84%
the different ways in which
a change, a polymorphism

00:27:33.868 --> 00:27:34.660 align:middle line:90%
can be transmitted.

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:38.020 align:middle line:84%
And I include this to
broaden your perspective.

00:27:38.020 --> 00:27:43.030 align:middle line:84%
Rather than getting entirely
fixated on the 3 million DNA

00:27:43.030 --> 00:27:45.940 align:middle line:84%
SNPs, let's broaden the
discussion here a little bit.

00:27:45.940 --> 00:27:48.220 align:middle line:84%
You can have not only
DNA polymorphisms,

00:27:48.220 --> 00:27:50.080 align:middle line:84%
but you can have
RNA polymorphisms,

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:51.730 align:middle line:90%
which are heritable.

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:54.970 align:middle line:84%
For example-- and I use
this an an extreme case--

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:57.460 align:middle line:90%
RNAi-- 22 nucleotide or so.

00:27:57.460 --> 00:27:59.650 align:middle line:84%
Probably a variety
of mechanisms.

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:02.680 align:middle line:90%


00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:07.280 align:middle line:84%
Bits of RNA can be
induced in various ways.

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:11.080 align:middle line:84%
And once induced, they can
replicate, essentially,

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:13.127 align:middle line:90%
within a cell and between cells.

00:28:13.127 --> 00:28:14.710 align:middle line:84%
They can spread
throughout an organism

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:18.730 align:middle line:84%
and probably be propagated
over generations

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:21.110 align:middle line:84%
between different
generations of organisms.

00:28:21.110 --> 00:28:23.830 align:middle line:90%
So this is heritable.

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:26.140 align:middle line:84%
And you can consider
it epigenetic

00:28:26.140 --> 00:28:28.780 align:middle line:84%
or genetic polymorphism,
depending on the nomenclature

00:28:28.780 --> 00:28:30.700 align:middle line:90%
that you adopt.

00:28:30.700 --> 00:28:35.350 align:middle line:84%
Even a protein conformation can
be considered a polymorphism

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:37.510 align:middle line:90%
that is heritable.

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:39.310 align:middle line:84%
The central dogma
tells us this protein

00:28:39.310 --> 00:28:42.340 align:middle line:84%
is encoded by a nucleic
acid, and there certainly

00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:44.050 align:middle line:90%
is still the case--

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:47.770 align:middle line:84%
even these heritable
polymorphisms and proteins.

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:49.330 align:middle line:84%
But it has a different
conformation,

00:28:49.330 --> 00:28:53.710 align:middle line:84%
and that conformation
recruits other conformations

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:56.530 align:middle line:84%
and so is inherited
not only within a cell,

00:28:56.530 --> 00:28:58.810 align:middle line:84%
but between cells
and between organisms

00:28:58.810 --> 00:29:04.660 align:middle line:84%
and is the causation of things
like mad cow disease and so on.

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:08.530 align:middle line:84%
And finally, modifications
of biopolymers,

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:11.830 align:middle line:90%
such as methylation, can occur.

00:29:11.830 --> 00:29:14.500 align:middle line:84%
And this is not formally
a DNA sequence change,

00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:20.650 align:middle line:84%
but it's a heritable
change that can

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:26.080 align:middle line:84%
have very significant effects
on things like cancer and gene

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:28.690 align:middle line:90%
expression in general.

00:29:28.690 --> 00:29:34.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is a broadening of
the definition of polymorphisms.

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:40.350 align:middle line:84%
And now let's talk
thoroughly about the ways

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:44.340 align:middle line:84%
that it can be inherited
as horizontal or vertical.

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:47.910 align:middle line:84%
Horizontal typically
means between species,

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:50.117 align:middle line:84%
but in a certain
sense, it could reflect

00:29:50.117 --> 00:29:52.200 align:middle line:84%
some of the things that
are going on here with RNA

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:55.590 align:middle line:84%
and protein inheritance in the
sense it is being horizontally

00:29:55.590 --> 00:29:58.770 align:middle line:84%
transmitted between different
cells within the same organism.

00:29:58.770 --> 00:30:01.830 align:middle line:84%
But generally, it's
a mechanism that

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:04.770 align:middle line:84%
does not involve
mitosis or meiosis

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:12.240 align:middle line:84%
of the nuclear or
organellar genomes.

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:15.150 align:middle line:84%
And the natural processes
are transduction

00:30:15.150 --> 00:30:19.320 align:middle line:84%
and transformation, being
distinguished by-- transduction

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:22.050 align:middle line:84%
typically involves
a viral or protein

00:30:22.050 --> 00:30:24.360 align:middle line:84%
coat for the nucleic
acid, and transformation

00:30:24.360 --> 00:30:27.180 align:middle line:84%
involves something closer
to naked nucleic acids.

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:32.100 align:middle line:84%
Transgenic is a completely
laboratory-based version

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:36.147 align:middle line:84%
of these two more
natural methods.

00:30:36.147 --> 00:30:37.980 align:middle line:84%
I think vertical we've
already talked about.

00:30:37.980 --> 00:30:41.700 align:middle line:84%
This is what we normally
think of inheritance.

00:30:41.700 --> 00:30:44.310 align:middle line:84%
Horizontal, we saw
in the tree of life,

00:30:44.310 --> 00:30:46.320 align:middle line:84%
is very common, even
in the late branches

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:47.460 align:middle line:90%
and the early branches.

00:30:47.460 --> 00:30:49.080 align:middle line:84%
Vertical, though,
is what we normally

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:52.620 align:middle line:84%
think about when we're doing
crosses in the laboratory.

00:30:52.620 --> 00:30:55.320 align:middle line:84%
Some of these are
maternally inherited,

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:58.320 align:middle line:84%
like mitochondria
and chloroplasts,

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:00.150 align:middle line:84%
but it's still the
same kind of process--

00:31:00.150 --> 00:31:04.602 align:middle line:90%
mitosis, segregation of DNA.

00:31:04.602 --> 00:31:06.060 align:middle line:84%
So now we've got
types of mutations

00:31:06.060 --> 00:31:08.310 align:middle line:84%
we want to talk about,
mutation drift and selection,

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:12.900 align:middle line:84%
as the main source
of the frequencies

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:15.400 align:middle line:90%
that we find in populations.

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:20.670 align:middle line:84%
We want to know, where do
allele frequencies come from?

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:23.970 align:middle line:84%
And I will maintain
that, generally speaking,

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:26.010 align:middle line:84%
in almost all living
systems, whether they're

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:29.520 align:middle line:84%
cells from organisms
that are mutating

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:33.840 align:middle line:84%
or whole organisms, whether they
do recombination or not, they

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:36.970 align:middle line:84%
will do mutation
selection and drift.

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:40.200 align:middle line:84%
Now, to develop a fairly
rigorous model here,

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:43.038 align:middle line:84%
yet simple, we have
some assumptions.

00:31:43.038 --> 00:31:44.580 align:middle line:84%
We always have
assumptions in models.

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:46.413 align:middle line:84%
If people tell you there
are no assumptions,

00:31:46.413 --> 00:31:49.990 align:middle line:84%
then you need to dig
a little further.

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:52.950 align:middle line:84%
The assumptions that we'll
make here for a little while--

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:54.960 align:middle line:84%
and then, I'll give
you a nice example

00:31:54.960 --> 00:31:58.305 align:middle line:84%
to undermine them all-- but
it's constant population size n.

00:31:58.305 --> 00:32:00.930 align:middle line:90%


00:32:00.930 --> 00:32:02.068 align:middle line:90%
You have random mating.

00:32:02.068 --> 00:32:04.110 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we were talking
about admixture before.

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:08.430 align:middle line:84%
Every member of the
population can randomly mate.

00:32:08.430 --> 00:32:10.780 align:middle line:84%
They're non-overlapping
generations.

00:32:10.780 --> 00:32:13.510 align:middle line:90%
This is a convenience.

00:32:13.510 --> 00:32:14.970 align:middle line:84%
We are not making
any assumptions

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:17.550 align:middle line:84%
about the population
allele frequencies

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:19.170 align:middle line:90%
being at equilibrium.

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:21.900 align:middle line:84%
Those of you have taken
biology courses with--

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:24.030 align:middle line:84%
Hardy-Weinberg makes
that assumption.

00:32:24.030 --> 00:32:25.950 align:middle line:84%
Here, this is a much
more general model.

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:28.800 align:middle line:84%
It includes non-equilibrium,
and equilibrium

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:30.810 align:middle line:90%
can be a special case.

00:32:30.810 --> 00:32:35.850 align:middle line:84%
So this is relatively
minor non-assumption.

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:37.380 align:middle line:84%
But we are assuming
that we do not

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:38.850 align:middle line:84%
have an infinite
number of alleles,

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:44.600 align:middle line:84%
nor an infinite number
of population size.

00:32:44.600 --> 00:32:48.710 align:middle line:84%
So now ignore
everything on the slide

00:32:48.710 --> 00:32:55.070 align:middle line:84%
but the upper left-hand
corner of slide 15 here.

00:32:55.070 --> 00:32:56.520 align:middle line:90%
This should look familiar.

00:32:56.520 --> 00:33:00.500 align:middle line:84%
This should look somewhat
like the logistic map

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:07.430 align:middle line:84%
where we had the incremental,
slow exponential increase

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:12.230 align:middle line:84%
of one allele in a
population over another,

00:33:12.230 --> 00:33:14.600 align:middle line:84%
or one organism
over another, based

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:16.370 align:middle line:84%
on the different
selection coefficients

00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:17.730 align:middle line:90%
of those organisms.

00:33:17.730 --> 00:33:19.760 align:middle line:84%
And this could be a
very small difference.

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:22.790 align:middle line:84%
Say, a 1% increase
per generation

00:33:22.790 --> 00:33:26.900 align:middle line:84%
will dominate after a
thousand generations

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:29.413 align:middle line:90%
or so, quite definitely.

00:33:29.413 --> 00:33:30.830 align:middle line:84%
And so that's what
you're seeing--

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:32.288 align:middle line:84%
the exponential
curve-- and then it

00:33:32.288 --> 00:33:38.210 align:middle line:84%
plateaus as it gets to
100% allele frequency.

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:40.730 align:middle line:84%
That's the full range
on the vertical axis,

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:43.220 align:middle line:90%
is 0% to 100% allele frequency.

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:48.980 align:middle line:84%
And generation goes
from 0 to 1,400.

00:33:48.980 --> 00:33:51.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, what it actually represents
in this particular case

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:54.075 align:middle line:84%
is closely related, a
little more complicated

00:33:54.075 --> 00:33:55.700 align:middle line:84%
than just one allele
replacing another,

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:59.810 align:middle line:84%
because here we have
diploids-- that is to say,

00:33:59.810 --> 00:34:01.910 align:middle line:84%
not a haploid that
just has one allele,

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:05.390 align:middle line:84%
like in the bacterial
species that we've implicitly

00:34:05.390 --> 00:34:07.280 align:middle line:90%
been talking about.

00:34:07.280 --> 00:34:10.139 align:middle line:84%
But you can have, here, now,
three combinations of alleles.

00:34:10.139 --> 00:34:19.010 align:middle line:84%
You can have, say, the
reference genotype of capital A,

00:34:19.010 --> 00:34:25.770 align:middle line:84%
capital A, which we'd
just call 100% fitness.

00:34:25.770 --> 00:34:28.170 align:middle line:84%
So we have fitness and
selection coefficient,

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:30.500 align:middle line:84%
which are interchangeable
terms that

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:34.469 align:middle line:84%
are-- very trivial mathematical
relationship between them.

00:34:34.469 --> 00:34:38.030 align:middle line:84%
So we'll use w and s in
different contexts here.

00:34:38.030 --> 00:34:40.310 align:middle line:84%
It just has population
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:40.310 --> 00:34:43.100 align:middle line:84%
Then, you can have big A, big A,
little a, little a, and big A,

00:34:43.100 --> 00:34:44.989 align:middle line:90%
little a as the heterozygote.

00:34:44.989 --> 00:34:46.940 align:middle line:84%
And we're assuming an
additive model here,

00:34:46.940 --> 00:34:50.300 align:middle line:84%
where you get a little
more selection with one

00:34:50.300 --> 00:34:52.950 align:middle line:84%
allele of little a, and
then two alleles of little

00:34:52.950 --> 00:34:54.230 align:middle line:90%
a results in 2s--

00:34:54.230 --> 00:34:57.020 align:middle line:90%
1 plus 2s.

00:34:57.020 --> 00:35:02.000 align:middle line:84%
And this has a very similar
curve to the logistic map,

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:05.590 align:middle line:84%
if you had simple
allele replacement.

00:35:05.590 --> 00:35:07.820 align:middle line:84%
And what you tend to
have in this population--

00:35:07.820 --> 00:35:10.250 align:middle line:84%
a thousand generations, in
the big scheme of things,

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:11.570 align:middle line:90%
may seem like a lot to you.

00:35:11.570 --> 00:35:14.520 align:middle line:84%
But in the big scheme, it's
a very short period of time,

00:35:14.520 --> 00:35:21.050 align:middle line:84%
and so you tend to have alleles
at frequency of 0% and 100%.

00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:23.330 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if you
have overdominant mode,

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:25.880 align:middle line:84%
where the heterozygote has the
highest fitness of the three

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:31.610 align:middle line:84%
possibilities, where 1 plus s
is larger than 1 or 1 plus t,

00:35:31.610 --> 00:35:33.650 align:middle line:90%
then you aim for equilibrium.

00:35:33.650 --> 00:35:37.400 align:middle line:84%
Whether you're starting at
close to 0% or close to 100%

00:35:37.400 --> 00:35:40.520 align:middle line:84%
the allele frequency will
converge on some equilibrium

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:41.900 align:middle line:90%
point--

00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:48.890 align:middle line:84%
in this case, somewhere
around 0.6 for one

00:35:48.890 --> 00:35:50.198 align:middle line:90%
allele and 0.4 for the other.

00:35:50.198 --> 00:35:51.240 align:middle line:90%
And it could be anywhere.

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:53.900 align:middle line:84%
It depends on the relative
fitnesses, s and t.

00:35:53.900 --> 00:35:56.990 align:middle line:90%


00:35:56.990 --> 00:35:59.180 align:middle line:84%
This is just a reminder
slide combining two slides

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:01.550 align:middle line:90%
from before.

00:36:01.550 --> 00:36:03.530 align:middle line:84%
That slide connects
the logistic map

00:36:03.530 --> 00:36:08.180 align:middle line:84%
from lecture 1 to the
selection coefficients

00:36:08.180 --> 00:36:12.210 align:middle line:84%
we've been talking about in
lecture 2 to the diploids

00:36:12.210 --> 00:36:14.210 align:middle line:84%
that we're mainly talking
about in this lecture,

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:15.650 align:middle line:90%
because humans are diploids.

00:36:15.650 --> 00:36:19.700 align:middle line:84%
And here, just connect
this to the fact

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:22.010 align:middle line:84%
that these selection
coefficients, s,

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:27.680 align:middle line:84%
or the fitness, w, is relevant
to different environments

00:36:27.680 --> 00:36:29.600 align:middle line:84%
and the different
times that organisms

00:36:29.600 --> 00:36:31.530 align:middle line:84%
spend in those
different environments.

00:36:31.530 --> 00:36:34.880 align:middle line:84%
And all mutants are tagged by
their DNA, and they're pooled,

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:36.860 align:middle line:84%
and they're selected,
and you can read them out

00:36:36.860 --> 00:36:39.120 align:middle line:90%
in a variety of methods.

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:41.900 align:middle line:84%
So now let's dig down into where
the allele frequencies come

00:36:41.900 --> 00:36:50.030 align:middle line:84%
from, based on
mutation or migration,

00:36:50.030 --> 00:36:53.580 align:middle line:84%
which we'll lump together here
as M, selection, and drift.

00:36:53.580 --> 00:36:58.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, the mutation will have
a forward rate constant

00:36:58.340 --> 00:36:59.240 align:middle line:90%
and a reverse.

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:01.490 align:middle line:84%
This should remind you
of the conversation

00:37:01.490 --> 00:37:05.070 align:middle line:84%
we just had about the
different kinds of alleles--

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:06.680 align:middle line:84%
the duplications
and deletions-- how

00:37:06.680 --> 00:37:09.890 align:middle line:90%
they can have different rates.

00:37:09.890 --> 00:37:11.990 align:middle line:84%
If they're tandem
duplication, then that

00:37:11.990 --> 00:37:14.480 align:middle line:90%
has a great tendency to delete.

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:18.260 align:middle line:84%
If it deletes down
to a single repeat,

00:37:18.260 --> 00:37:20.010 align:middle line:84%
there's now no
longer any repeat,

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:24.050 align:middle line:84%
and so the chance of generating
the exact duplicate is low.

00:37:24.050 --> 00:37:29.210 align:middle line:84%
So the frequency of forward
and reverse mutation

00:37:29.210 --> 00:37:32.760 align:middle line:84%
is represented by f
and r, respectively.

00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:33.955 align:middle line:90%
Now, what we'll see--

00:37:33.955 --> 00:37:35.330 align:middle line:84%
we're going to
walk through this.

00:37:35.330 --> 00:37:39.290 align:middle line:84%
Starting with a
frequency, t sub i,

00:37:39.290 --> 00:37:47.520 align:middle line:84%
where i is the number of
mutants in a population size n.

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:52.800 align:middle line:84%
So here, down at the bottom,
is i mutants in a population

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:53.460 align:middle line:90%
sized n.

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:56.700 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see that
applying the mutation,

00:37:56.700 --> 00:37:58.950 align:middle line:84%
applying the selection,
and applying the drift

00:37:58.950 --> 00:38:02.040 align:middle line:84%
are all applications of
binomial distributions

00:38:02.040 --> 00:38:04.350 align:middle line:84%
when we're talking about
this discrete population of n

00:38:04.350 --> 00:38:06.120 align:middle line:90%
individuals.

00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:09.180 align:middle line:84%
Remember the three bell-shaped
curves [INAUDIBLE]??

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:12.060 align:middle line:90%
Curves that can be bell-shaped.

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:15.210 align:middle line:84%
Here, it's clearly a
discrete population

00:38:15.210 --> 00:38:20.310 align:middle line:84%
because we have n individuals,
taking i mutants at a time.

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:22.370 align:middle line:84%
So the binomials
that we'll be using--

00:38:22.370 --> 00:38:25.500 align:middle line:84%
all three processes
have the same form,

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:28.500 align:middle line:84%
where you have a combination
of some population, n,

00:38:28.500 --> 00:38:30.870 align:middle line:90%
and a subpopulation, i.

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:34.650 align:middle line:84%
And of course, the
remainder is n minus i.

00:38:34.650 --> 00:38:39.720 align:middle line:84%
And the different combinations
are times some probability,

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:45.840 align:middle line:84%
because probability is the last
parameter in the binomial here.

00:38:45.840 --> 00:38:49.480 align:middle line:84%
A binomial is a function
of n, i, and p--

00:38:49.480 --> 00:38:51.840 align:middle line:84%
the population size,
subpopulation size,

00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:53.130 align:middle line:90%
and some frequency.

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:56.190 align:middle line:84%
It can be either a forward or
reverse mutation frequency.

00:38:56.190 --> 00:38:59.190 align:middle line:84%
It can be a selection
probability,

00:38:59.190 --> 00:39:00.600 align:middle line:84%
or it can be a
drift probability.

00:39:00.600 --> 00:39:02.830 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see how each
of these work out.

00:39:02.830 --> 00:39:06.780 align:middle line:90%
So we start with a frequency.

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:13.140 align:middle line:84%
You can have i ranges
from 0 to If i is 0,

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:15.720 align:middle line:84%
then the frequency
is 0 over n, or 0.

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:18.960 align:middle line:84%
If i is n, it's n over
n, or frequency is 100%.

00:39:18.960 --> 00:39:23.460 align:middle line:84%
Just that same vertical axis
we had on all previous slides.

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:26.880 align:middle line:84%
The starting frequencies have
some distribution, t sub i,

00:39:26.880 --> 00:39:29.190 align:middle line:90%
for i going from 0 to n.

00:39:29.190 --> 00:39:35.040 align:middle line:84%
And now we want to derive a
new vector of frequencies,

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:36.600 align:middle line:90%
which would be m.

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:39.720 align:middle line:84%
And all we do is we apply
the binomial distribution

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:42.750 align:middle line:84%
for the forward process
or forward mutation.

00:39:42.750 --> 00:39:45.330 align:middle line:84%
And then, once we're
done with that,

00:39:45.330 --> 00:39:50.700 align:middle line:84%
we now use the
m's and adjust it.

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:53.670 align:middle line:84%
So now, give it a chance
to do the reverse mutation,

00:39:53.670 --> 00:39:56.880 align:middle line:84%
because you'll generate this
binomial distribution forwards,

00:39:56.880 --> 00:40:00.720 align:middle line:84%
and then you'll give
them a chance to revert.

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:02.190 align:middle line:90%
Then, you apply selection.

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:04.740 align:middle line:90%
A new binomial.

00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:10.500 align:middle line:84%
Now, here, the probability of
a transition from your mutants,

00:40:10.500 --> 00:40:12.660 align:middle line:84%
starting with t--
then, you go to m.

00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:15.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, to get to s, the
transition probability's

00:40:15.210 --> 00:40:17.070 align:middle line:90%
a function of this fitness.

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:20.190 align:middle line:84%
Remember, w and s,
fitness and selection.

00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:25.560 align:middle line:84%
Here, the fitness
determines the probability

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:29.760 align:middle line:84%
of a transition in a
binomial distribution.

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:32.373 align:middle line:84%
And there are two slightly
different equations

00:40:32.373 --> 00:40:34.290 align:middle line:84%
that you use, depending
on whether the fitness

00:40:34.290 --> 00:40:37.650 align:middle line:84%
is greater than
1 or less than 1,

00:40:37.650 --> 00:40:40.860 align:middle line:84%
whether it has a
tendency to increase

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:44.100 align:middle line:84%
with time, due to
selection, or to decrease

00:40:44.100 --> 00:40:47.050 align:middle line:84%
that allele with time, as
a function of selection.

00:40:47.050 --> 00:40:49.690 align:middle line:84%
And that's what these
two cases are here.

00:40:49.690 --> 00:40:53.040 align:middle line:84%
And then, finally, after
you've applied forward

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:54.930 align:middle line:84%
and inverse mutation
and selection,

00:40:54.930 --> 00:40:59.170 align:middle line:84%
whether it's more fit or less
fit, then you apply drift.

00:40:59.170 --> 00:41:01.695 align:middle line:90%
Now, drift just means that--

00:41:01.695 --> 00:41:05.260 align:middle line:90%


00:41:05.260 --> 00:41:05.760 align:middle line:90%
think of it.

00:41:05.760 --> 00:41:08.700 align:middle line:84%
If you have a small
population of individuals--

00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:13.740 align:middle line:84%
let's say they're a set of
colored balls in a jar in front

00:41:13.740 --> 00:41:14.330 align:middle line:90%
of us, here.

00:41:14.330 --> 00:41:16.920 align:middle line:84%
It's a small number, and
I pull a handful out,

00:41:16.920 --> 00:41:18.750 align:middle line:84%
because in each
generation, you're

00:41:18.750 --> 00:41:20.400 align:middle line:84%
going to duplicate
the population.

00:41:20.400 --> 00:41:23.190 align:middle line:84%
But we remember the assumption
of constant population size.

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:26.490 align:middle line:84%
If I pull out the new
generation and forget

00:41:26.490 --> 00:41:27.990 align:middle line:84%
about the rest of
the duplicated,

00:41:27.990 --> 00:41:29.388 align:middle line:84%
they could all be
the same color.

00:41:29.388 --> 00:41:31.680 align:middle line:84%
And that's the chance that
you could drift to fixation,

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:35.310 align:middle line:84%
where one of them now dominates,
not because it's superior,

00:41:35.310 --> 00:41:37.500 align:middle line:84%
from a selection
standpoint, not because it's

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:41.220 align:middle line:84%
been mutated in a directed
way to that point,

00:41:41.220 --> 00:41:43.320 align:middle line:84%
but just because of
random drift that you

00:41:43.320 --> 00:41:46.500 align:middle line:90%
have a constant population.

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:48.180 align:middle line:84%
And so you can see
how that would depend

00:41:48.180 --> 00:41:50.010 align:middle line:90%
on how many are in the jar.

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:51.960 align:middle line:84%
If I take half of
them out of the jar,

00:41:51.960 --> 00:41:55.770 align:middle line:84%
and the jar only has five in it,
then it's a much higher chance

00:41:55.770 --> 00:41:58.710 align:middle line:84%
that we'll go to fixation
quickly than if the jar has

00:41:58.710 --> 00:41:59.550 align:middle line:90%
millions in it.

00:41:59.550 --> 00:42:01.860 align:middle line:84%
If I take half a
million out, it's

00:42:01.860 --> 00:42:04.620 align:middle line:84%
very likely that it will
still be more or less the same

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:05.410 align:middle line:90%
ratios.

00:42:05.410 --> 00:42:07.560 align:middle line:84%
And this is exactly
how it plays out

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:10.740 align:middle line:84%
when you look at
random genetic drift.

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:13.450 align:middle line:84%
It's very dependent
on population size.

00:42:13.450 --> 00:42:16.120 align:middle line:84%
So here are simulations
going out to, say,

00:42:16.120 --> 00:42:19.110 align:middle line:84%
150 generations on the
horizontal axis and the allele

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:22.240 align:middle line:84%
frequency, as usual,
going from 0% to 100%.

00:42:22.240 --> 00:42:25.980 align:middle line:84%
And we start with a
population with 50/50 ratio

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:26.715 align:middle line:90%
of two alleles.

00:42:26.715 --> 00:42:29.820 align:middle line:90%


00:42:29.820 --> 00:42:32.310 align:middle line:84%
And what you see is,
if the population,

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:37.620 align:middle line:84%
the number of individuals, is
only 25, then you quickly fix.

00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:41.040 align:middle line:90%
Maybe 30 generations, you can--

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:43.810 align:middle line:90%
and this is anecdotal--

00:42:43.810 --> 00:42:45.580 align:middle line:90%
get fixation.

00:42:45.580 --> 00:42:49.240 align:middle line:84%
As it goes up to a
population size of 2,500,

00:42:49.240 --> 00:42:51.890 align:middle line:84%
you can see it, for all
intents and purposes, is flat.

00:42:51.890 --> 00:42:53.410 align:middle line:90%
It will eventually fix.

00:42:53.410 --> 00:42:56.830 align:middle line:84%
I assure you, this simulation--
if you run it long enough,

00:42:56.830 --> 00:42:58.660 align:middle line:84%
one of the two alleles
will go to zero,

00:42:58.660 --> 00:43:01.168 align:middle line:84%
and the other one
will go to 100%.

00:43:01.168 --> 00:43:02.710 align:middle line:84%
And you do another
simulation, and it

00:43:02.710 --> 00:43:05.085 align:middle line:84%
might be the other one, because
this is random drift, not

00:43:05.085 --> 00:43:05.700 align:middle line:90%
selection.

00:43:05.700 --> 00:43:08.240 align:middle line:90%


00:43:08.240 --> 00:43:10.180 align:middle line:84%
So you can see the
final frequencies are

00:43:10.180 --> 00:43:13.750 align:middle line:84%
going to be some complicated
function of mutation rates

00:43:13.750 --> 00:43:18.550 align:middle line:84%
and selection rates and drift
rates, which, in that last one,

00:43:18.550 --> 00:43:21.190 align:middle line:84%
is a function of
population size.

00:43:21.190 --> 00:43:23.622 align:middle line:84%
When the population size
is very, very large,

00:43:23.622 --> 00:43:25.580 align:middle line:84%
you can see that's going
to be constant enough.

00:43:25.580 --> 00:43:28.060 align:middle line:84%
That's why you can see
very subtle differences

00:43:28.060 --> 00:43:31.515 align:middle line:90%
in selection coefficient.

00:43:31.515 --> 00:43:33.890 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're going to come back
to the mutation, selection,

00:43:33.890 --> 00:43:36.220 align:middle line:84%
and drift in the context
of human genetics in just

00:43:36.220 --> 00:43:36.740 align:middle line:90%
a moment.

00:43:36.740 --> 00:43:41.200 align:middle line:84%
But first, I want to tell
you the last component

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:44.600 align:middle line:84%
of population genetics that
we'll be talking about,

00:43:44.600 --> 00:43:45.760 align:middle line:90%
which is recombination.

00:43:45.760 --> 00:43:48.580 align:middle line:84%
Now, this doesn't occur in
every biological system.

00:43:48.580 --> 00:43:52.280 align:middle line:84%
I made the argument before
that the mutation, selection,

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:55.390 align:middle line:84%
and drift occurs in
every biological system,

00:43:55.390 --> 00:43:59.410 align:middle line:84%
from cells to
organisms of all types.

00:43:59.410 --> 00:44:01.270 align:middle line:84%
In those biological
systems where

00:44:01.270 --> 00:44:06.790 align:middle line:84%
DNA can be exchanged by
transduction, transformation,

00:44:06.790 --> 00:44:13.090 align:middle line:84%
meiotic fusion, and so forth,
then you can get recombination.

00:44:13.090 --> 00:44:15.880 align:middle line:84%
And these two figures
illustrate that.

00:44:15.880 --> 00:44:19.570 align:middle line:84%
On the left-hand
side of slide 19

00:44:19.570 --> 00:44:22.780 align:middle line:84%
is the non-recombination
scenario,

00:44:22.780 --> 00:44:25.000 align:middle line:84%
and on the right-hand
side is the

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:29.800 align:middle line:84%
sexual, or
recombination-mediated, change

00:44:29.800 --> 00:44:31.070 align:middle line:90%
in gene combinations.

00:44:31.070 --> 00:44:32.230 align:middle line:90%
So let's look at this.

00:44:32.230 --> 00:44:34.540 align:middle line:90%
Time is going horizontally.

00:44:34.540 --> 00:44:38.350 align:middle line:84%
And you can see, at the very
left-hand side, the beginning

00:44:38.350 --> 00:44:41.860 align:middle line:84%
of each of these scenarios,
you get a certain rate

00:44:41.860 --> 00:44:43.790 align:middle line:90%
of occurrence of mutations.

00:44:43.790 --> 00:44:45.490 align:middle line:84%
This is based on
the forward mutation

00:44:45.490 --> 00:44:48.520 align:middle line:90%
rate in our previous equations.

00:44:48.520 --> 00:44:50.110 align:middle line:90%
And they occur very early on.

00:44:50.110 --> 00:44:51.670 align:middle line:90%
A, B, and C all occur.

00:44:51.670 --> 00:44:56.560 align:middle line:84%
But they tend to die
out because of drift.

00:44:56.560 --> 00:45:00.550 align:middle line:84%
And the population size is
small enough that half of them

00:45:00.550 --> 00:45:03.610 align:middle line:90%
die out and one of them fixes.

00:45:03.610 --> 00:45:07.090 align:middle line:84%
And A is destined to fix, but it
takes some time for it to fix.

00:45:07.090 --> 00:45:10.750 align:middle line:90%


00:45:10.750 --> 00:45:14.080 align:middle line:84%
It starts out at a frequency
close to 0, and by the time

00:45:14.080 --> 00:45:17.230 align:middle line:84%
it's 50% to 100%, it's
ready to start picking up

00:45:17.230 --> 00:45:19.103 align:middle line:84%
a second mutation at
the same frequency they

00:45:19.103 --> 00:45:20.020 align:middle line:90%
were occurring before.

00:45:20.020 --> 00:45:23.890 align:middle line:84%
And now you can pick up the B
mutation while the C dies off.

00:45:23.890 --> 00:45:25.720 align:middle line:90%
It comes and goes due to drift.

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:28.490 align:middle line:84%
And then, finally,
once AB fixes,

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:30.580 align:middle line:84%
then you can pick up
the C, and you get ABC.

00:45:30.580 --> 00:45:33.820 align:middle line:84%
That was a long,
slow serial process.

00:45:33.820 --> 00:45:35.920 align:middle line:84%
On the right-hand
side, we see what

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:38.710 align:middle line:84%
can happen in the
case of exchange

00:45:38.710 --> 00:45:42.730 align:middle line:84%
of genetic material in a
really mixing population

00:45:42.730 --> 00:45:46.450 align:middle line:84%
now that A, B, and C all
occur at the same frequency

00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:47.440 align:middle line:90%
as on the left.

00:45:47.440 --> 00:45:49.810 align:middle line:84%
But now, because they're
exchanging information

00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:54.400 align:middle line:84%
very early on, before B has a
chance to die off due to drift,

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:57.580 align:middle line:84%
it combines its DNA
with A, and you get AB.

00:45:57.580 --> 00:45:59.705 align:middle line:84%
And some of the
small A population

00:45:59.705 --> 00:46:01.900 align:middle line:84%
is just destined
to be fixed anyway,

00:46:01.900 --> 00:46:05.470 align:middle line:84%
but happens to combine
with C. And again,

00:46:05.470 --> 00:46:13.690 align:middle line:84%
before drift can wipe them
out, the very small selection

00:46:13.690 --> 00:46:15.970 align:middle line:84%
that you have that
couldn't overcome drift

00:46:15.970 --> 00:46:20.140 align:middle line:84%
in the left-hand
panel now fixes them

00:46:20.140 --> 00:46:23.980 align:middle line:84%
all, the really favorable
combination of A, B, and C,

00:46:23.980 --> 00:46:25.370 align:middle line:90%
very early on.

00:46:25.370 --> 00:46:29.470 align:middle line:84%
And so there's a whole series of
arguments and counterarguments

00:46:29.470 --> 00:46:31.210 align:middle line:84%
in the population
genetics literature

00:46:31.210 --> 00:46:32.900 align:middle line:90%
about why is there sex.

00:46:32.900 --> 00:46:36.250 align:middle line:84%
Of course, we all know
have our own reasons.

00:46:36.250 --> 00:46:39.220 align:middle line:84%
But in here, they say
there's a huge cost.

00:46:39.220 --> 00:46:42.700 align:middle line:84%
The counterargument
is there's a huge cost

00:46:42.700 --> 00:46:44.650 align:middle line:90%
of having two genders--

00:46:44.650 --> 00:46:48.940 align:middle line:84%
maybe as much as 50%,
possibly higher--

00:46:48.940 --> 00:46:52.240 align:middle line:84%
because they have different
morphologies and different

00:46:52.240 --> 00:46:55.400 align:middle line:90%
capabilities and so on.

00:46:55.400 --> 00:46:57.190 align:middle line:90%
But then, there's this benefit.

00:46:57.190 --> 00:46:58.840 align:middle line:84%
This is the risk,
and the benefit

00:46:58.840 --> 00:47:00.490 align:middle line:90%
is the earlier recombination.

00:47:00.490 --> 00:47:02.770 align:middle line:84%
But then there's
counterarguments and so forth

00:47:02.770 --> 00:47:05.680 align:middle line:90%
that we won't go into.

00:47:05.680 --> 00:47:06.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:47:06.610 --> 00:47:08.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Going back to drift.

00:47:08.050 --> 00:47:09.790 align:middle line:90%
The drift is actually random.

00:47:09.790 --> 00:47:13.680 align:middle line:84%
What is it that causes
either allele to fix?

00:47:13.680 --> 00:47:16.840 align:middle line:84%
If you have two different
alleles [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:16.840 --> 00:47:20.650 align:middle line:84%
zero for one allele, why
wouldn't it just [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:20.650 --> 00:47:21.340 align:middle line:90%
come back?

00:47:21.340 --> 00:47:22.600 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Well, it can.

00:47:22.600 --> 00:47:24.790 align:middle line:84%
You can see here
that it's starting

00:47:24.790 --> 00:47:29.020 align:middle line:84%
to head so that it's
fixing on one allele,

00:47:29.020 --> 00:47:31.930 align:middle line:84%
and it changes direction,
and it fixes the other one.

00:47:31.930 --> 00:47:37.210 align:middle line:84%
So it can go all the way
down to close to zero

00:47:37.210 --> 00:47:39.130 align:middle line:90%
and then bounce back up.

00:47:39.130 --> 00:47:41.925 align:middle line:84%
This is just some
typical simulations here.

00:47:41.925 --> 00:47:43.300 align:middle line:84%
If you do enough
of these, you'll

00:47:43.300 --> 00:47:45.577 align:middle line:90%
find every possible behavior.

00:47:45.577 --> 00:47:47.910 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So what you're
saying-- basically, eventually,

00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:49.810 align:middle line:84%
if you just give it
enough time, it will fix.

00:47:49.810 --> 00:47:50.768 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: It will fix.

00:47:50.768 --> 00:47:54.485 align:middle line:84%
You can't necessarily predict
which one will it fix on.

00:47:54.485 --> 00:47:56.110 align:middle line:84%
If there's no selection,
half the time,

00:47:56.110 --> 00:47:57.568 align:middle line:84%
it'll fix on one,
half on the other

00:47:57.568 --> 00:48:01.950 align:middle line:84%
if they start out at
the frequency of 50/50.

00:48:01.950 --> 00:48:04.242 align:middle line:84%
But you have to think of it
in the context of mutation

00:48:04.242 --> 00:48:06.450 align:middle line:84%
and selection, too, because
they're all acting there.

00:48:06.450 --> 00:48:09.240 align:middle line:84%
And when you say that something
is selectively neutral,

00:48:09.240 --> 00:48:10.840 align:middle line:90%
what you really mean--

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:14.160 align:middle line:84%
everything has some very, very
tiny selective coefficient.

00:48:14.160 --> 00:48:18.360 align:middle line:84%
But if it's tiny enough
and the population size

00:48:18.360 --> 00:48:20.880 align:middle line:84%
is small enough, then
drift will blind you,

00:48:20.880 --> 00:48:22.120 align:middle line:90%
and you can't see it.

00:48:22.120 --> 00:48:24.027 align:middle line:84%
But if you get really
big populations,

00:48:24.027 --> 00:48:25.860 align:middle line:84%
then you'll get small
drift, and then you'll

00:48:25.860 --> 00:48:28.170 align:middle line:90%
see more subtle selection.

00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:31.125 align:middle line:90%


00:48:31.125 --> 00:48:34.260 align:middle line:84%
The human population
is very large.

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:37.500 align:middle line:84%
Some of the oceanic
species are truly enormous.

00:48:37.500 --> 00:48:40.810 align:middle line:84%
So you need to think about
that as a possibility.

00:48:40.810 --> 00:48:44.340 align:middle line:84%
So now let's talk
about common diseases.

00:48:44.340 --> 00:48:49.470 align:middle line:84%
The question is, are
common diseases really--

00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:51.690 align:middle line:84%
to what extent are they
caused by common variants?

00:48:51.690 --> 00:48:54.120 align:middle line:84%
Clearly, some of them are
caused by common variants.

00:48:54.120 --> 00:48:56.640 align:middle line:84%
And we've said,
well, common variants

00:48:56.640 --> 00:49:01.140 align:middle line:84%
really shouldn't be deleterious,
because they would be wiped out

00:49:01.140 --> 00:49:03.960 align:middle line:90%
by selection.

00:49:03.960 --> 00:49:07.050 align:middle line:84%
They could be very, very mildly
deleterious such that drift

00:49:07.050 --> 00:49:10.680 align:middle line:84%
will cause them to get
fixated or persist,

00:49:10.680 --> 00:49:14.910 align:middle line:84%
but they can't be
really noticeably.

00:49:14.910 --> 00:49:17.400 align:middle line:84%
Selection coefficients
of 10 to the minus 4--

00:49:17.400 --> 00:49:19.170 align:middle line:90%
very, very tiny effects--

00:49:19.170 --> 00:49:26.620 align:middle line:84%
can be wiped out in
normal-sized populations.

00:49:26.620 --> 00:49:30.990 align:middle line:84%
So here are three examples
of common variants

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:33.300 align:middle line:84%
that almost certainly
are associated

00:49:33.300 --> 00:49:34.900 align:middle line:90%
with common diseases.

00:49:34.900 --> 00:49:37.620 align:middle line:84%
So why are these special
cases, in my opinion,

00:49:37.620 --> 00:49:38.880 align:middle line:90%
rather than the general case?

00:49:38.880 --> 00:49:40.995 align:middle line:84%
APOE4 I earlier alluded
to is associated

00:49:40.995 --> 00:49:42.210 align:middle line:90%
with Alzheimer's dementia.

00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:46.180 align:middle line:84%
It's involved in lipid
transport and metabolism.

00:49:46.180 --> 00:49:51.450 align:middle line:84%
And this particular
allele, the E4 allele,

00:49:51.450 --> 00:49:54.270 align:middle line:90%
is present at 20% in humans.

00:49:54.270 --> 00:49:58.950 align:middle line:84%
About 80% is the other allele,
the second most common allele

00:49:58.950 --> 00:50:02.230 align:middle line:90%
is the E3 allele.

00:50:02.230 --> 00:50:05.990 align:middle line:84%
And so you might say, well,
this bad allele, this E4,

00:50:05.990 --> 00:50:11.640 align:middle line:84%
is the one that should be
present in the common ancestors

00:50:11.640 --> 00:50:14.300 align:middle line:90%
of humans.

00:50:14.300 --> 00:50:16.260 align:middle line:84%
The E3, the good one,
should be present

00:50:16.260 --> 00:50:20.760 align:middle line:84%
in the common ancestors, and
this E4 is a recent aberration.

00:50:20.760 --> 00:50:23.460 align:middle line:84%
It's somehow getting
into human population.

00:50:23.460 --> 00:50:27.510 align:middle line:84%
But actually, the E4 is the
ancestral one, presumably

00:50:27.510 --> 00:50:29.070 align:middle line:84%
due to some
difference in diet or

00:50:29.070 --> 00:50:33.210 align:middle line:90%
some other selective effects.

00:50:33.210 --> 00:50:37.030 align:middle line:84%
The E4, which is currently
bad for us, we think,

00:50:37.030 --> 00:50:42.130 align:middle line:84%
was really good for some
of our related species.

00:50:42.130 --> 00:50:45.930 align:middle line:84%
And so we need to think very
carefully before we do anything

00:50:45.930 --> 00:50:48.420 align:middle line:84%
drastic about eliminating
this allele from, say,

00:50:48.420 --> 00:50:51.990 align:middle line:90%
the human population.

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:56.880 align:middle line:84%
Hemoglobin sickle cell,
the sickle cell allele,

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:00.030 align:middle line:84%
is probably the
oldest and most famous

00:51:00.030 --> 00:51:04.830 align:middle line:84%
of the molecularly
characterized alleles.

00:51:04.830 --> 00:51:09.330 align:middle line:84%
Zuckerland and Pauling made
this famous many decades ago.

00:51:09.330 --> 00:51:13.450 align:middle line:84%
And it exists in 17% of
the human population,

00:51:13.450 --> 00:51:18.790 align:middle line:84%
and this is responsible for
oxygen transport in red blood

00:51:18.790 --> 00:51:19.290 align:middle line:90%
cells.

00:51:19.290 --> 00:51:21.240 align:middle line:84%
And you saw in one
of the earlier slides

00:51:21.240 --> 00:51:23.970 align:middle line:84%
today those sickle-shaped
cells, which

00:51:23.970 --> 00:51:27.510 align:middle line:84%
have a huge effect on the
hemodynamics of the red blood

00:51:27.510 --> 00:51:28.650 align:middle line:90%
cell.

00:51:28.650 --> 00:51:32.250 align:middle line:84%
Well, another red blood cell
component, an enzyme, G6PD,

00:51:32.250 --> 00:51:36.720 align:middle line:84%
which is involved in maintaining
the redox function in the cell,

00:51:36.720 --> 00:51:38.740 align:middle line:90%
is as high as 40%.

00:51:38.740 --> 00:51:42.780 align:middle line:84%
The mutant, whichever one you
want-- they're so close to 50%.

00:51:42.780 --> 00:51:47.640 align:middle line:84%
They're just two
alleles, and one of them

00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:53.250 align:middle line:84%
is deleterious, in a certain
sense, a biochemical sense.

00:51:53.250 --> 00:51:58.410 align:middle line:84%
But both of these have
a heterozygote advantage

00:51:58.410 --> 00:52:00.510 align:middle line:90%
in being malaria-resistent.

00:52:00.510 --> 00:52:02.010 align:middle line:84%
And so probably
that's the reason

00:52:02.010 --> 00:52:03.510 align:middle line:84%
that it's common
in the population.

00:52:03.510 --> 00:52:09.570 align:middle line:84%
And this is probably proving
to be the rule that these

00:52:09.570 --> 00:52:11.790 align:middle line:84%
are examples of that
convergence that we

00:52:11.790 --> 00:52:14.400 align:middle line:84%
saw in the case of
balanced polymorphisms

00:52:14.400 --> 00:52:16.750 align:middle line:84%
where the heterozygote
has some advantage,

00:52:16.750 --> 00:52:19.980 align:middle line:84%
and so you get a balanced
point rather than one

00:52:19.980 --> 00:52:23.880 align:middle line:84%
or the other dominating
through drift or selection.

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:26.280 align:middle line:84%
And the third example,
which we will develop

00:52:26.280 --> 00:52:30.300 align:middle line:90%
in much more detail, is CCR5.

00:52:30.300 --> 00:52:32.220 align:middle line:84%
There's a deletion
of 32 base pairs

00:52:32.220 --> 00:52:35.070 align:middle line:84%
which confers resistance
to one of the greatest

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:38.970 align:middle line:84%
plagues in the history
of the human race, which

00:52:38.970 --> 00:52:41.100 align:middle line:90%
is the AIDS virus.

00:52:41.100 --> 00:52:44.250 align:middle line:84%
And its frequency
is 9% in Caucasians.

00:52:44.250 --> 00:52:49.830 align:middle line:84%
I think we wish that it were
100% when we worry about HIV,

00:52:49.830 --> 00:52:53.160 align:middle line:84%
but we need to wonder
why it's not 100%.

00:52:53.160 --> 00:52:57.120 align:middle line:84%
There may be some
other reason for it

00:52:57.120 --> 00:53:00.750 align:middle line:84%
being a nondeleted
version in so many humans,

00:53:00.750 --> 00:53:02.170 align:middle line:90%
and we need to understand that.

00:53:02.170 --> 00:53:06.270 align:middle line:84%
And as far as I know, we
don't understand that.

00:53:06.270 --> 00:53:10.110 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is the last
slide before the break.

00:53:10.110 --> 00:53:12.690 align:middle line:90%


00:53:12.690 --> 00:53:14.790 align:middle line:84%
I promised you
that we would take

00:53:14.790 --> 00:53:18.960 align:middle line:84%
that simple mathematical
treatment of mutation selection

00:53:18.960 --> 00:53:22.500 align:middle line:84%
and drift and show that it
actually has some impact,

00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:25.890 align:middle line:84%
that it's actually
used in human genetics.

00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:30.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, I must warn you that
this is not a consensus view.

00:53:30.960 --> 00:53:32.790 align:middle line:84%
This is a view that
I find appealing,

00:53:32.790 --> 00:53:38.310 align:middle line:84%
in slide 21, that Jon
Pritchard has authored.

00:53:38.310 --> 00:53:43.470 align:middle line:84%
And he titled it in
a provocative way--

00:53:43.470 --> 00:53:47.010 align:middle line:84%
Are Rare Variants Responsible
for Susceptibility

00:53:47.010 --> 00:53:49.090 align:middle line:90%
to Complex Diseases?

00:53:49.090 --> 00:53:51.150 align:middle line:90%
And this is a quote.

00:53:51.150 --> 00:53:52.890 align:middle line:84%
It's customary in
theoretical work

00:53:52.890 --> 00:53:58.470 align:middle line:84%
relating to complex diseases
that the allele frequencies

00:53:58.470 --> 00:54:00.460 align:middle line:84%
are treated as
parameters of the model.

00:54:00.460 --> 00:54:03.570 align:middle line:84%
And typically in
models, you'll have

00:54:03.570 --> 00:54:07.830 align:middle line:84%
derived values and
parameters, which

00:54:07.830 --> 00:54:11.820 align:middle line:84%
are input, the things that the
user is expected to provide.

00:54:11.820 --> 00:54:16.140 align:middle line:84%
And you don't want to have to be
guessing at allele frequencies

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:17.520 align:middle line:90%
and having those parameters.

00:54:17.520 --> 00:54:22.960 align:middle line:84%
So what's new here is that,
using an evolutionary process,

00:54:22.960 --> 00:54:25.620 align:middle line:84%
which includes-- you guessed
it-- selection, mutation,

00:54:25.620 --> 00:54:29.460 align:middle line:84%
and genetic drift, we
can learn or, as I say,

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:31.620 align:middle line:84%
model the underlying
allele frequencies.

00:54:31.620 --> 00:54:35.400 align:middle line:84%
They can be derived rather than
being required as an input.

00:54:35.400 --> 00:54:38.460 align:middle line:90%


00:54:38.460 --> 00:54:45.900 align:middle line:84%
And this is illustrated
here with a model.

00:54:45.900 --> 00:54:50.340 align:middle line:84%
This is entirely theoretical,
but the parameters

00:54:50.340 --> 00:54:53.790 align:middle line:84%
that are used are based on some
genetic studies that have been

00:54:53.790 --> 00:54:55.890 align:middle line:90%
done, for example, on autism.

00:54:55.890 --> 00:54:59.460 align:middle line:84%
And so let me just define
some of the terms here.

00:54:59.460 --> 00:55:03.510 align:middle line:84%
This risk ratio is related,
in a certain sense,

00:55:03.510 --> 00:55:05.460 align:middle line:84%
to the selection and
fitness coefficients

00:55:05.460 --> 00:55:06.370 align:middle line:90%
we were talking about before.

00:55:06.370 --> 00:55:07.995 align:middle line:84%
Now, selection and
fitness coefficients

00:55:07.995 --> 00:55:10.110 align:middle line:90%
refer to reproductive fitness.

00:55:10.110 --> 00:55:14.310 align:middle line:84%
And in human genetics,
we have broader interests

00:55:14.310 --> 00:55:17.460 align:middle line:84%
into all sorts of things
that either don't affect

00:55:17.460 --> 00:55:19.770 align:middle line:84%
reproductive fitness,
or we don't know

00:55:19.770 --> 00:55:21.030 align:middle line:90%
how it affects reproduction.

00:55:21.030 --> 00:55:23.220 align:middle line:84%
But it affects some
kind of medical

00:55:23.220 --> 00:55:26.250 align:middle line:90%
or just some trait of interest.

00:55:26.250 --> 00:55:31.050 align:middle line:84%
In this case, the risk
ratio replaces the selection

00:55:31.050 --> 00:55:31.660 align:middle line:90%
coefficient.

00:55:31.660 --> 00:55:36.330 align:middle line:84%
And this is basically saying, my
brother or sister has autism--

00:55:36.330 --> 00:55:38.140 align:middle line:90%
if they did--

00:55:38.140 --> 00:55:41.460 align:middle line:84%
then I would have a
75-fold higher chance

00:55:41.460 --> 00:55:45.270 align:middle line:84%
of having it than
someone selected randomly

00:55:45.270 --> 00:55:48.220 align:middle line:84%
from the same population
as I come from.

00:55:48.220 --> 00:55:54.120 align:middle line:84%
So the 70-fold increase is
a very high heritability

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:57.390 align:middle line:90%
for this particular example.

00:55:57.390 --> 00:56:00.090 align:middle line:84%
Also in this example,
through some genetics

00:56:00.090 --> 00:56:03.180 align:middle line:84%
we won't discuss,
it seemed reasonable

00:56:03.180 --> 00:56:08.640 align:middle line:84%
that the number of loci involved
might be a large number,

00:56:08.640 --> 00:56:10.143 align:middle line:90%
on the order of 100 or so.

00:56:10.143 --> 00:56:12.060 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of a
lot of common diseases.

00:56:12.060 --> 00:56:15.160 align:middle line:84%
When you start listing the
well-characterized ones,

00:56:15.160 --> 00:56:16.680 align:middle line:84%
we start listing
the number of genes

00:56:16.680 --> 00:56:19.610 align:middle line:84%
that either are known or
are plausible, like cancer

00:56:19.610 --> 00:56:22.970 align:middle line:84%
or walking down the
street, or whatever.

00:56:22.970 --> 00:56:26.600 align:middle line:84%
These really complicated traits
involve a very large number

00:56:26.600 --> 00:56:31.040 align:middle line:84%
of cell types, a large
number of cell components,

00:56:31.040 --> 00:56:33.330 align:middle line:84%
and hence a large
number of genes.

00:56:33.330 --> 00:56:36.210 align:middle line:84%
So here, they've assumed
a model with 100 loci,

00:56:36.210 --> 00:56:38.990 align:middle line:90%
100 different genes.

00:56:38.990 --> 00:56:44.300 align:middle line:84%
And they have multiplicative
effects of the polymorphisms

00:56:44.300 --> 00:56:47.270 align:middle line:90%
in those genes between loci.

00:56:47.270 --> 00:56:51.800 align:middle line:84%
That is to say, you need to
have gene 1 working and gene

00:56:51.800 --> 00:56:54.290 align:middle line:84%
2 and gene 3, so
that's multiplicative.

00:56:54.290 --> 00:56:57.000 align:middle line:84%
But then, the polymorphisms
you introduced into a gene--

00:56:57.000 --> 00:56:58.580 align:middle line:84%
you could think there are
a lot of genes involved,

00:56:58.580 --> 00:57:00.830 align:middle line:84%
and there are a lot of different
positions within the gene that

00:57:00.830 --> 00:57:02.990 align:middle line:84%
could be involved, and
each of those is additive.

00:57:02.990 --> 00:57:08.840 align:middle line:84%
You have a little reduction in
activity due to first mutation,

00:57:08.840 --> 00:57:10.580 align:middle line:84%
and then a second
mutation, a third.

00:57:10.580 --> 00:57:13.560 align:middle line:84%
Each of those is this
or this or this or this,

00:57:13.560 --> 00:57:15.240 align:middle line:84%
and so that's an
additive effect.

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:18.620 align:middle line:84%
And so they have various
historical justifications

00:57:18.620 --> 00:57:21.380 align:middle line:84%
for having additive
penetrance within the gene

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:25.400 align:middle line:84%
and multiplicative across
different loci, different genes

00:57:25.400 --> 00:57:27.210 align:middle line:90%
in the genome.

00:57:27.210 --> 00:57:33.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, for these 100 loci,
there will be a top five

00:57:33.530 --> 00:57:36.950 align:middle line:84%
that affect the
relative risk the most,

00:57:36.950 --> 00:57:41.645 align:middle line:84%
and that's what's been
plotted here, where we have--

00:57:41.645 --> 00:57:44.480 align:middle line:90%


00:57:44.480 --> 00:57:48.110 align:middle line:84%
the zero curve, you
see, is in the absence

00:57:48.110 --> 00:57:51.800 align:middle line:84%
of any of these
multiplicative effects.

00:57:51.800 --> 00:57:54.680 align:middle line:84%
It's as if you had no loci that
were affecting the frequency

00:57:54.680 --> 00:57:56.100 align:middle line:90%
of susceptibility alleles.

00:57:56.100 --> 00:58:01.640 align:middle line:84%
And so, as in most
unselective populations,

00:58:01.640 --> 00:58:07.490 align:middle line:84%
you'll have most of the
alleles being either

00:58:07.490 --> 00:58:10.010 align:middle line:90%
at zero frequency or 100%.

00:58:10.010 --> 00:58:12.860 align:middle line:84%
Basically, the zero
representing the absence

00:58:12.860 --> 00:58:15.290 align:middle line:90%
of the other the alternatives.

00:58:15.290 --> 00:58:16.860 align:middle line:90%
The 100% is there.

00:58:16.860 --> 00:58:24.230 align:middle line:84%
But if you have these
top five loci out of 100

00:58:24.230 --> 00:58:26.150 align:middle line:84%
that contribute
to the risk ratio,

00:58:26.150 --> 00:58:29.810 align:middle line:84%
then those are represented
by these four curves.

00:58:29.810 --> 00:58:31.610 align:middle line:84%
The frequency of
susceptibility alleles,

00:58:31.610 --> 00:58:35.180 align:middle line:84%
which range along the horizontal
axis now, from 0 to 100%,

00:58:35.180 --> 00:58:38.145 align:middle line:84%
have what he calls a
probability density.

00:58:38.145 --> 00:58:39.770 align:middle line:84%
It's clearly not a
probability density,

00:58:39.770 --> 00:58:41.720 align:middle line:84%
because it's not going
to integrate to 1,

00:58:41.720 --> 00:58:50.330 align:middle line:84%
but here, it's the probability,
the histogram of risk ratios.

00:58:50.330 --> 00:58:58.820 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a break,
and we'll drill down

00:58:58.820 --> 00:59:01.475 align:middle line:84%
on the association study
in a very interesting case.

00:59:01.475 --> 00:59:04.580 align:middle line:90%


00:59:04.580 --> 00:59:10.340 align:middle line:84%
Any problem sets to hand in can
put them here during the break.

00:59:10.340 --> 00:59:20.020 align:middle line:90%


00:59:20.020 --> 00:59:26.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I understand about
modeling mutation selection.

00:59:26.490 --> 00:59:31.000 align:middle line:90%